#N canvas 0 0 760 1580 10;
#X text 12 8 opl3-player - an OPL3 (YMF262 \, the Sound Blaster 16 FM chip) VGM/VGZ file through vgm~ + opl3~ (every chip channel + the PCM scoped);
#X text 12 40 vgm~ outlet 1 carries the non-YM2612 chips of the log: `opl <reg> <data>` register writes (YMF262 port 0/1) ride the same outlet as the PSG bytes - connect opl3~ there (and sn76489~ for the PSG of mixed files: each object picks its own selector);
#X text 12 100 the console load line reports which chips a file declares \, e.g. "chips: opl3 14318180 Hz (outlet 1 (opl))" - entries marked "not played" have no object in this build;
#X text 12 140 opl3~ outlets: 0 = left sum \, 1 = right sum (the chip's own stereo mix - reg 0xC0 pan bits per channel!) \, 2..19 = chip channels 1-18 alone (pan-independent). WIRE BOTH SUMS - a log can place whole phrases left-only or right-only \, so one outlet alone drops those notes. Each sum has its own gain here\; dense 18-channel mixes can reach ~8 over +-1 \, the final gain owns the headroom;
#X text 12 220 mono option: send `mono 1` to make both sums carry everything (ignores the pan bits - handy for a mono chain);
#X text 12 250 opl3~ also speaks the ym2612~ voice layer: `note <midi> [vel]` \, `chN note` \, gate (inlet 2) \, `alg/fb/pan/op/gm` \, `chN gm <1-128>` (Mobilnik-v2 GM bank built in \, `bank <file.wopl>` loads others) - raw reg writes still own everything for vgm playback;
#X text 12 310 demo_opl3.vgm ships with the examples - an arpeggio on chip channel 1. Put your own .vgm/.vgz next to a patch or anywhere on the pd search path.;
#X msg 40 350 load demo_opl3.vgm;
#X msg 40 378 play;
#X msg 100 378 pause;
#X msg 160 378 stop;
#X msg 40 406 loop 1;
#X msg 110 406 info;
#X msg 180 406 reset;
